---
// /docs/install — the plugin install and its one requirement.
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/docs-layout/DocsLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "../../../lib/base.ts";
---

<DocsLayout
  current="install"
  title="Install"
  description="Install Agentry as a Claude Code plugin in two commands, then set it up with /agentry:init. OpenCode: global or per-project, pinned. Node ≥ 24."
  eyebrow="Getting Started · Install"
  lede="Two commands install the plugin; a third sets it up for your repo. That is all you need to start."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2 class="prose__h">Install the plugin</h2>
    <p>
      Agentry installs as a Claude Code plugin from its GitHub repo. From a Claude Code session in your repo, add it, install it, and let <code>/agentry:init</code> draft its process file from what it finds:
    </p>
    <pre class="flag"><code><span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>/plugin marketplace add silentthreadstudio/agentry#v0.4.2
<span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>/plugin install agentry@agentry-dev
<span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>/agentry:init</code></pre>
    <p>
      That's the whole setup. The conducting front door (<code>/agentry:go</code>), the SDLC
      specialists, and the durable memory layer all run from here — entirely from the terminal,
      no account and no build step. Head to <a href={withBase("docs/quickstart/")}>Quickstart</a>
      to hand it a first task.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <code>#v0.4.2</code> suffix pins the install to the 0.4.2 release; the repo's main branch moves with
      development. To move to a later release, remove the marketplace and add it again with the new tag. Each
      release is also on <a href="https://github.com/silentthreadstudio/agentry/releases">GitHub Releases</a>
      as a zip.
    </p>

    <h2 class="prose__h">OpenCode</h2>
    <h3 class="prose__h">What you are installing</h3>
    <p>One npm package, <code>agentry-opencode</code>, used in two ways:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>OpenCode loads it as a plugin.</strong> <code>opencode plugin add</code> writes an entry in <code>opencode.jsonc</code>, and OpenCode fetches its own copy into its own cache. That copy is not on your PATH.</li>
      <li><strong>You install or run it as the CLI.</strong> <code>npm install -g agentry-opencode</code> (or <code>npx agentry-opencode</code>) gives you the <code>agentry</code> command, which the agents run in the shell. The same package carries the agents, commands and skills that <code>agentry init</code> writes for OpenCode to read.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>So the plugin and the CLI are the same package installed twice, for two readers: OpenCode, and your shell.</p>
    <p>The plugin gives the agents native tools, with structured input and output and OpenCode's permission prompt on writes:</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>agentry_board</code></td><td>Reads the board: status, check, explain, contract, next-id, commits, scan, draft list, instructions.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>agentry_board_write</code></td><td>Changes the board: add, set, fix, draft. Previews unless --apply is passed.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>agentry_lane</code></td><td>Reads lanes: session start, the sweep plan, a lane's ledger (exec show).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>agentry_lane_drive</code></td><td>Drives lanes: exec steps, merge, sweep --apply, note, spike, session close.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>agentry_mem</code></td><td>Reads memory: recall, search, stats.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>agentry_mem_write</code></td><td>Changes memory: write, update, forget, feedback, episode.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Without the plugin the agents fall back to running <code>agentry ...</code> in the shell, so everything still works.
      The CLI is for the same board, lane and memory operations from your terminal or the agents' shell, plus <code>init</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
      An OpenCode setup has three pieces. The package, <code>agentry-opencode</code>, is on npm and needs OpenCode V2
      and Node 24 or newer.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Piece</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>CLI</td><td><code>agentry</code>, which the agents run in the shell.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Agents, commands and skills</td><td>Files OpenCode reads.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Plugin</td><td>The native <code>agentry_board</code>, <code>agentry_lane</code> and <code>agentry_mem</code> tools, loaded from OpenCode's config. Recommended; without it, the agents fall back to the CLI.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Pick one install.</p>

    <h3 class="prose__h">Global install</h3>
    <p>Recommended for one person across all repos. Run these in this order:</p>
    <pre class="flag"><code><span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>npm install -g agentry-opencode
<span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>opencode plugin add agentry-opencode@0.4.2
<span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>opencode service restart
<span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>agentry init --host opencode --global --apply</code></pre>
    <p>
      Then, in each repo, run <code>/agentry/init</code> in OpenCode to learn or scaffold the board, and run
      <code>agentry init --host opencode --apply</code> to add the <code>AGENTS.md</code> block. Run that repo init without
      <code>--vendor</code>; its payload overrides the global one. <code>--global</code> writes only agents, commands and
      skills, never touches a repo, and never removes your own files there.
    </p>

    <h3 class="prose__h">Per-project install</h3>
    <p>Pins the version in the repo and can be shared by committing <code>.opencode/</code>:</p>
    <pre class="flag"><code><span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>opencode plugin add agentry-opencode@0.4.2
<span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>opencode service restart
<span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>npx agentry-opencode@0.4.2 init --host opencode --vendor --apply</code></pre>
    <p>
      Then run <code>/agentry/init</code> in OpenCode, and run the same <code>npx agentry-opencode@0.4.2 init --host opencode --vendor --apply</code>
      again to add the <code>AGENTS.md</code> block. <code>--vendor</code> copies the CLI into the repo's <code>.agentry/</code>,
      so nothing global is needed.
    </p>

    <h3 class="prose__h">Pin the plugin version</h3>
    <p>
      Write <code>agentry-opencode@&lt;version&gt;</code> in <code>opencode plugin add</code>, never <code>@latest</code>:
      OpenCode caches the version it first fetched for <code>@latest</code> and does not refetch it, so an update silently
      does nothing. Keep exactly one <code>agentry-opencode</code> entry in <code>opencode.jsonc</code>, because running
      <code>plugin add</code> again adds a second line.
    </p>

    <h3 class="prose__h">Updating</h3>
    <p>Global:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Edit the version in the existing <code>"agentry-opencode@&lt;version&gt;"</code> line in <code>~/.config/opencode/opencode.jsonc</code>, or run <code>opencode plugin add agentry-opencode@&lt;new&gt;</code> and delete the old line.</li>
      <li><code>opencode service restart</code></li>
      <li><code>npm install -g agentry-opencode@&lt;new&gt;</code></li>
      <li><code>agentry init --host opencode --global --apply</code></li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Per project: rerun <code>npx agentry-opencode@&lt;new&gt; init --host opencode --vendor --apply</code> in the repo.
      Init previews changes, rewrites only Agentry's generated files, keeps your own files, and does nothing when already in sync.
    </p>

    <h3 class="prose__h">Check it worked</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><code>agentry --version</code> prints <code>0.4.2</code>.</li>
      <li><code>opencode debug agents</code> lists <code>agentry/conductor</code> and the 8 specialists.</li>
      <li>In OpenCode, typing <code>/agentry</code> shows the commands. Commands are namespaced: <code>/agentry/init</code>, not <code>/agentry:init</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Without <code>--apply</code>, init previews. The <code>AGENTS.md</code> block waits for a learned process, so a first run
      in a repo skips it and says so.
    </p>

    <div class="note">
      <span class="note__key">Requires</span>
      <span>Node ≥ 24 — the memory engine uses Node's built-in <code>node:sqlite</code>, so there are no native modules to compile.</span>
    </div>

    <div class="prose__cta">
      <p>
        Next: <a href={withBase("docs/permissions/")}>Permissions</a> covers how a tool call gets
        approved.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
